  {
    "episode_id": 48869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think the two are inextricably linked. America's ability to project its influence and power in the world is a direct result of its economic power, its economic strength, its military capability, its will, its value system, its ability to project those values. And, you know, your question almost, for those who say look inward because of such problems, it almost suggests that if we look inward, the problems of the world wouldn't affect us in some way, which is absolutely contrary. We've learned the hard way. that the best way to promote and protect America's interests at home are to project American strength and influence abroad. And I mean that in a very humble sense. When I say project American influence and values, I don't mean impose America's values on others, America's influence on others. But I think that I still call me a hopeless romantic. I still believe that America is that shining city on a hill that President Reagan talked about. I believe that America is the best hope for the world and the American values, really the model for the world. And so we have a lot of problems at home, make no mistake. But I also think that we have the most potential to be a source of strength and inspiration for others. And that's honestly why I've decided to run for political office because I think having leadership in our government that thinks about America that way and projects those values is really important.",
