    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "years. 106,000 across the United States last year alone death. So this is a huge problem. And that doesn't even account for the economic burden, the thousands of people in rehab, what is done to families, communities. It's just it's been wretched and a huge problem. And it's the direct consequence of a number of things of China making basic ingredients for fentanyl and shipping them to Mexico. The direct consequence of Mexican cartels assembling those ingredients into fentanyl. It's the direct consequence of border policies and domestic policies that have made it easier for illegal immigrants to cross the border at actual border crossings or in illegal border crossings that have brought fentanyl into the United States and once it crosses the border in 48 hours, it's found its way into northeastern part of our country where Pennsylvania is. So this is an issue where we need to interdict And ban those in fentanyl ingredients coming from China. That's a huge, what should, in my opinion, be a huge part of our foreign policy and our engagement with China. It's a place where I think we need to put enormous pressure on the cartels in Mexico, hopefully in partnership with the Mexican government. And it's a place where I think a set of border policies need to be in place that make it much more difficult and interdict dangerous poison coming into our borders. And then, of course, we need domestic policy at home. that helps deal with the hopelessness that lead people to rely on opioids and fentanyl and deals with the addiction challenges we have. So it's a multi-faceted set of policies, but it's a perfect example of if you were sitting in the capital one of our adversaries tomorrow and you said, hey, how would we really give America a hard time? You might invent something called fentanyl. find ways to get it into our country, into the bloodstream of our country, and kill 100,000 Americans a year with a growing risk of this crisis firing out of control. It's a big deal and it needs to be addressed on multiple levels rather.",
