    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, I think, you know, you have to step back and say and acknowledge that America, and it was bipartisan really made a bet 30 years ago relating to China 35 years ago, which is which was to say, if we brought China into the global economy, if we encourage that reaffirmed it with WTO accession with permanent normal trade relations and so forth, that we would create a global trading environment where there would be free and fair trade, where China's markets would open, America would benefit from doing commerce with China, benefit from China doing business with America, that China would abide by the rules in terms of intellectual property protections and so forth. And what happened is over time, there's been a clear divergence where China hasn't followed the rules. And China hasn't lived up to the expectations that went into that original calculus. And so increasingly, U.S. economic interests and China's economic interests have diverged. Even though there's benefits of that economic relationship, those benefits have been disproportionate. to China and the standards by which China is operated have not been consistent with the promise. But those interests diverged much more dramatically what President Xi came into power and I guess it was 2000 and 2012. And we see now that China's vision for its future, Xi's vision is one of China being a techno-authoritarian regime that displaces America as the primary superpower. That is the vision. That's not something I'm speculating on. That's in his speeches. And Xi lays out the 20 most important technologies in the world that says China must be the leader in all of these technologies. And so we now have a growing divergence where we have to fundamentally rethink our economic relationship and our national security relationship with China. And so that's why in my book and superpower in peril and in my keystone agenda, for America, I essentially lay out how we should deal with China. And what I say is we should reduce our dependency on China in critical sectors like semiconductors and pharmaceuticals, things that matter are at the core of America's economic strength. We can't be dependent on China and China's supply chains. And simultaneously, and we can't trust that they won't steal the emotional property. And simultaneously, we cannot invest and let American companies or investors in China in a way that is supporting the growth of its military, this techno-authoritarian regime, the Chinese Communist Party. And that's a stark departure. from the policies we've had in the preceding decades. And it's something I think needs to happen immediately.",
