  {
    "episode_id": 48869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think I'd say make a couple reflections on that. First, our debt situation in America is increasingly becoming unsustainable. We have $34 trillion of debt. Our interest payment on our debt alone is $1 trillion a year. I looked this the other day just to get a sense of it physically. A trillion dollars a year is 168 40 foot trailers, filled to the top of $100 bills. Just to put this in perspective, the enormity of our debt and just the interest payments on the debt is truly unsustainable. And so, as a matter of public policy, we've got to reign in discretionary spending. We've got to, in my opinion, pull back on many of these excessive spending programs that have gone into place in recent years of trillions of dollars of incremental spending and subsidization. And we need to, and we need to actually deal with the fundamental drivers of our debt problem. And we need to have pro-growth economic policies that stimulate growth, which is part of the way you deal with the debt. So I think our kids are not going to forgive us for the burden we're leaving, and then people have been saying that for years, but the scope and scale of it is clearly reaching a tipping point. Now, there's two other observations I'd make though. One is that But because we've issued so much debt, others have had to buy debt. Foreign countries around the world, as you said, China, Japan, and many others. And we're now seeing, as the fiscal situation in America becomes more precarious, we're seeing those foreign buyers pull back. And we've seen that in recent treasury auctions. And that's a real problem. So it's a canary in the coal mine of our fiscal situation being a bit unsustainable. The holders of that debt You know, it's a symbiotic relationship with America because it's in, you know, it's in nobody's interest for America to default on that debt, not America's interest, not the holders of that debt. So I think the much more worrisome thing is that future purchases of debt appear to be trailing off. But I'll say a final thing that may seem in contradiction to this, which is when you think of a global economy, you're not thinking about it in absolute terms, you're thinking about it compared to everybody else. And America's economy with all the challenges it has, and it has many challenges, including political dysfunction in terms of dealing with policy. But the American economy still stands out compared to all the others. So if you could have our economy or China's economy, China's economy is in deep trouble. You'd have our economy. If you could have us or Europe, you'd pick us. If you could have us or Japan, you'd pick America. With all of our challenges, our economy still is fundamentally quite strong. But the most important thing about the American economy is its resilience using",
