    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, why I didn't answer that question last time. I now realize that in negotiation, the thing I learned in dealing with Chinese when I was in the government was they didn't follow the rules. It was clear. even in the 2008-2009 timeframe when I was in the government dealing with the Chinese that they were diverging from their obligations. And as the point I made earlier, that just got a lot worse. In dealing with China, from the private sector perspective, Bridgewater wasn't an investor in China, relatively modest investor. But the thing that you see is this economy is very much a state controlled economy. State run enterprises are part of the big investors in the technology sector and the military sector. And that creates all sorts of distortions from a market perspective. And so that is part of the reason that China is unable to respond to some of these economic factors",
