    "turn_text": "Absolutely, absolutely, because American strength in the world is a sort of stabilizing force. And when adversaries around the world think America is weak, ham-handed, slow to respond, that encourages aggression. And I think it is important to mention Afghanistan before I go to Ukraine, because Afghanistan laid a predicate. And it was one of the reasons I got involved politically. It was such an incompetent One can debate intellectually whether we should have been at state and Afghanistan or not. I'm not making that point. When we decided to withdraw from Afghanistan, the incompetent way that that happened, the surprise that it presented to our allies, the Afghani supporters falling out of the wheel wells of our jet, 13 service members being killed, it was a debacle. And it sent a signal to the world, wait a second, that's not the America we know. The same was true with Ukraine, where I think had the messages been clear, I think it's less likely that Putin would have invaded. President Biden approved the Nord Stream 2 pipeline, which was certainly an indication to Russia that the consequences of its purported aggression were not going to be economically biting. Second, when the actual attacks happened, what was the first response to Biden administration to send a plane to help Zelensky escape? as opposed to stand by Ukraine with the kind of military support he needed right out of the gate, not dribs and drabs of military support over time. But the support he needed right in the fight, you know, as a soldier, I",
