  {
    "episode_id": 48869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 38,
    "turn_text": "You know, you know, when you're in the middle of a firefight, you don't need the weapons three months later. You need the weapons. Now you need to support now when the fight is and when you're at the beginning and the core of the fight. And so at every step along the way, I think the Biden administration has been slow to respond. And now The support of the American people is understandably slipping away. And I think the final failure is the inability to envision the inability to project the inability to lay out how this ends. So America is tired of endless wars. to our benefit, we're not sending American soldiers into Ukraine, which I would not support, but we're giving military support, but the presence failed to lay out how this additional incremental spending of billions of American taxpayer dollars at a time when our economy's struggling, some of that money going to your Ukrainian bureaucrats as opposed to a military weaponry, the presence failed to lay out how this will lead to some sort of peaceful end to the conflict. I think it's a terribly tragic situation because we, you know, having poop suppression successful in Europe, I think, runs counter to America's interests. I think it says it's a danger signal to China, but the presidents failed to execute this war in a way that I think either could have deterred it in the first place or made it much more effective on the part of the ingredients.",
