  {
    "episode_id": 48869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 46,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, you know, across campuses, I think in many places we've seen kids you know, marching, kids calling for a genocide of Israelis. In many places, including the University of Pennsylvania, we've had Jewish students hiding in their dormitory rooms because they didn't feel safe walking across campuses. And you say to yourself, how could that be? How is that possible? And then you watch those three university presidents testifying and you go, oh, that's how it's possible because there's a lack of moral clarity, a lack of appreciation, a lack of the importance of an institution creating an environment where all of its students feel safe. And in that particular instance, it was like the ideology of the oppress versus the oppressors that sort of hijacked their brains. And they had lost the basic thread of decency and morality and the difference between right and wrong. And so I had called for Liz McGill's resignation long before that testimony because I was watching this and seeing that he was really not exercising any leadership at all. And her response had been very mealy mouth and I thought not reflective of the kind of leadership that was required. Now, what can what can a senator do? What can our leaders do? Well, you know, there's a huge part of our universities that are in particular private universities by the fact that their endowments are treated as tax-free. And so, you know, that's questionable. These have these enormous endowments, and if they're going to be in places which are or perpetrating a set of values or inconsistent with what's reasonable, inconsistent with the kind of moral leadership we require, that should be called into question. The same with federal grants. So I think the financial support that these universities get is maybe a starting point. And I want to be clear on this. I'm not advocating a particular political position. I'm not advocating conservative versus liberal. I'm, I'm advocating universities being a place where people can truly exchange ideas and that all ideas or that all reasonable ideas are met with. an openness. And that's clearly not happened. And I think it's a real tragedy. And I think this was one of those situations where, as you know, I'm a graduate of Princeton, I'm very proud to have been at Princeton and loved it. But most universities, not all, but most have shown to be the be the king without clothes in this situation and I think it's a moment for real reflection among the trustees and the leadership of these universities.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Private universities receive tax-exempt treatment due to their endowments.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "In many locations, students have marched and called for the genocide of Israelis.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Universities should serve as spaces for open exchange of all reasonable ideas.",
        "confidence": 0.91
