  {
    "episode_id": 48869,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 48,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, if you think about throughout America's short history, because America is relatively young in the history of other countries, we have been unique in our capacity to innovate in radical ways that have really changed the world. The PC was a really good example of this nuclear vision and other space travel satellites. You go back through history, America has been at the forefront. And I don't think that's an accident. I think that has a lot to do with the idea that it was a nation conceived with the notion of individual liberty and freedom, being the organizing mechanism for America. And that sense of individualism has led and that sense that the private sector, not the government, should govern the allocation of capital and that merit. Marriage should dictate who gets that capital. Marriage should dictate those ideas and the idea that failure is an embedded part of American society and an embedded part of our capital system. Those are all the basic building blocks that have led to America being an innovation machine. And so the first thing we need to do is not screw that up, not have a government that's becoming more and more socialist and where the government's allocating capital disproportionately, not have a place where individual freedom is increasingly restricted. I talked to businesses across Pennsylvania, whether it's dairy farmers or manufacturers or beauty salons, and the degree to which the government is more and more involved in regulating is becoming on the present and it's a huge problem for most businesses. So that's the core. Let's not lose the thing that made us special. That's the starting point. And then some of these new technologies are really transcendent. These new technologies are not military technologies, civilian technologies. They are at the intersection of all those things. Artificial intelligence",
