    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48869.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 52,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, I have so many great members, but let me say too real quickly. You know, as I said, there was I wasn't I wasn't academically that confident when I first came to Princeton and A couple professors really changed my life. One of them was Aaron Freeberg, who I mentioned. The other was Dick Oman. And Dick was an elderly gentleman. And when I gave him my first paper, I got a comment back. He would grade it in leaded pencil. And then I think it had a B on top of it. But when you go through the text, it had A, B, and little marks, C, D, all the way through the alphabet. And then when you go through the alphabet, at the end of the paper, it would be double A, double B. And then you got to the end of the paper, and there was a set of type comments that were as long as the paper itself about the quality of the writing, the quality of the thinking. And it was such a lesson in care, a lesson in investment. I don't know how much he did that with other people, but he certainly invested in me as, I think, at an affinity for former military officers. And through him, I gained, I became a much better writer. I gained my intellectual confidence. He encouraged me eventually to apply for the PhD program, which I did, and I was successful. He became my squash partner. And by the way, he was a staunch Democrat and would have been opposed to certainly some of my political views in, and I suspect many of my political views now. But the dearest, most committed um a friend and mentor and professor and so the less I mean I've changed my life uh you know there's been a handful of people in in my life that have made it all the difference and dick almond was one of them uh and it was it was really special and I I think there's probably a lot of prince and uh graduates who could point to somebody like that who changed their who changed their life and uh and I'm uh will be forever grateful for that",
